<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Graticula.Console.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- D-60: one database-backed suite at a time. See the file for why the
         run settings could not do this and why the lock is a file handle. -->
    <Compile Include="..\shared\OneSuiteAtATime.cs" Link="OneSuiteAtATime.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>


  <!-- <b>No browser automation package, and that is a decision rather than an
       omission.</b> Playwright and Selenium both do far more than this suite
       needs, and both add a browser download step to a repository whose CI
       already has Chrome on the runner. What is actually needed is: launch a
       browser, evaluate an expression in a page, read the answer back. Chrome
       speaks that over a WebSocket, and `System.Net.WebSockets.ClientWebSocket`
       is in the framework. DevTools.cs is that: 300 lines of code, most of it
       launching a browser and finding the debugging port it chose. This comment
       said *ninety* until they were counted — a small instance of the habit
       tools/conditions.py exists to break, left here rather than quietly fixed.

       The trade is stated so it can be revisited: this client has no waiting
       primitives, no selector engine and no other browser. If the suite grows to
       need any of them, adopting a package is the right answer and this comment
       is the record of when it stopped being. -->

  <!-- <b>No project reference, for the same reason the conformance suite has
       none.</b> The subject here is a page loaded over HTTP into a real browser;
       reaching for our own types would let a test assert against a constant the
       server also reads. Everything below is a selector, a string, or a computed
       style — which is all a person at the keyboard has. -->

</Project>
